## INTRODUCTION
As a clinically approved treatment modality, photodynamic therapy (PDT) uses a photosensitizer (PS) that can absorb light and convert tissue oxygen into reactive oxygen species (ROS) such as ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ to kill tumor cells.1,2 Nevertheless, the hydrophobic nature and insufficient tumor selectivity of most porphyrin derivatives and its analogs, the main PSs in clinical use, limited PDT efficacy and may cause the phototoxicity to adjacent normal tissues.3,4 Most recently, porphyrinic metal-organic frameworks (MOFs)5-10 have emerged as an attractive nanophotosensitizers (nPSs) for PDT.11-19 MOFs-based nPSs were directly self-assembled from porphyrin PSs and metal ions/clusters through coordination interactions, thus resulting in high PS loading capacity without self-quenching.11 The pores of MOFs also facilitate the ROS diffusion, leading to high PDT efficacy over other nPSs.11-19 However, most MOFs-based PDT operates predominantly by visible light, which limits the PDT efficiency severely as a result of shallow depth of tissue penetration. Moreover, hypoxia, a distinct pathophysiological feature in most types of solid tumors, presents a significant challenge to PDT due to oxygen-dependent ROS generation.20- 25 In addition, PDT itself can induce acute hypoxia due to the
continuous oxygen consumption in local microenvironment, further blunting PDT efficacy.22-25
Lanthanide-doped upconversion nanoparticles (UCNPs) that can convert low energy near-infrared (NIR) light to high-energy ultraviolet (UV)/visible light have received much attention in a variety of biomedical fields.26-31 Recently, owning to their fascinating photo-upconversion ability, UCNPs were explored as transducers for NIR light-mediated photoregulation of neural activity,32,33 biosensing,34-36 as well as biomedicine.37-40 In particular, there has been an increasing interest in the utilization of UCNPs for improved, deeper-penetrating PDT, in which UV/Vis light produced by UCNPs upon NIR light irradiation can in turn excite PSs for the generation of ROS.41-46 Since the efficient energy transfer from UCNP to PS is necessary to achieve NIR light-induced PDT, the attachment of sufficient amount of PSs to the UCNP in close proximity is of prime importance for efficient ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ generation. In the past decade, three common approaches (i.e., mesoporous silica coating, physical adsorption, covalent conjugation) have been developed to attach PSs on the surface of UCNPs.41-46 However, these strategies are often limited by i) sophisticated materials syntheses and multistep modification processes, ii) low PS loading efficiency and minimal PS loading content, and iii)
limited ${}^{1}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ generation efficacy due to inefficient energy transfer from UCNPs to PSs.
Recently, we reported the synthesis of a heterodimer composed of porphyrinic MOF and UCNP to achieve NIR light-activated production of ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ .47 However, the energy transfer from the UCNP to porphyrins in the MOF domain is inefficient because of the long distance between the two types of nanoparticles (NPs) within the asymmetric heterodimer.
Moreover, the using of such systems as nPSs for PDT is still limited by tumor hypoxia. Thus motivated, here we report the synthesis of a core-shell heterostructure consisting of an individual UCNP as the core and porphyrinic MOFs as the shell for enhanced antitumor activity of combined PDT, hypoxiaactivated chemotherapy and immunotherapy (Figure 1). We discover the dominant role of surface engineering of UCNPs for mediation of the growth of UCNP-MOF heterostructures. Compared with UCNP-MOF heterodimers (UCDs), the UCNP@MOF core-shell nanostructures (UCSs) showed much higher efficacy of NIR light-activated ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ generation because of higher energy transfer efficiency from the UCNP core to MOF shell. On the other hand, in comparison with aforementioned conventional methods for the attachment of PSs on the surface of UCNPs, our one-step approach is much simpler and allows for a high loading efficiency and content of PSs. Furthermore, taking advantages of the porous structure of MOFs, a hypoxia-activatable prodrug (tirapazamine, TPZ) was loaded into the nanopores of MOF shell in UCSs for a highly effective synergistic cancer therapy. TPZ can be stimulated by the hypoxic tumor microenvironment to generate toxic oxidizing radical species through a single-electron reduction reaction, resulting in a higher toxicity to hypoxic cancer cells than that in normal cells. Moreover, we demonstrated that the combination of the nanoplatform (TPZ/UCSs) with $\mathfrak{a}$ -PD-L1 checkpoint-blockade therapy not only eradicate primary sites but also inhibit untreated distant tumors through systemic antitumor immunity.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
The synthetic routes for UCSs and UCDs are presented in Figure 2a. The synthesis of the heterostructures starts with the preparation of highly uniform core-shell structured UCNPs with an average diameter of ${\sim}46\times8~\mathrm{nm}$
and a shell thickness of ${\sim}5\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 1b and Figure S1). The UCNPs were then modified with citrate acid (CA) or polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP) to mediate the growth of MOFs on their surface. The zeta potential values of PVP-modified UCNPs (PVP-UCNPs) and CA-modified UCNPs (CA-UCNPs) are $+13.8~\mathrm{\mV}$ and $-4.7~\mathrm{\mV}$ , respectively (Figure S2). Interestingly, the usage of UCNPs with different surface ligands for the synthesis resulted in heterostructures of different morphologies (Figure 2c and d). For the synthesis with PVPUCNPs as seeds, the asymmetric UCNP-MOF heterodimers are observed (Figure 2c, Figure S3), while UCNP@MOF core-shell structures were achieved when CA-UCNPs were used as seeds for MOFs growth (Figure 2d, Figure S3). The size of the MOF NPs in UCDs ranges from 38 to $65~\mathrm{nm}$ , while the thickness of the MOF shell in UCSs is around $8\mathrm{nm}$ . Further evidence for the formation of UCDs and UCSs is provided by the investigation with EDS elemental mapping images (Figure 2e and f) and line scan (Figure $2\mathrm{g}$ and h) obtained from high-angle annular darkfield scanning transmission electron microscopy (HAADFSTEM). The crystal structure of UCDs and UCSs was investigated by a powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) technique (Figure 2i). The PXRD pattern displayed typical peaks corresponding to the cubic phase of PCN-224 MOFs and hexagonal phase of ${\mathrm{NaGdF}}_{4}$ , confirming the phase purity and high crystallinity of the two types of heterostructures. The controlled synthesis of the nanostructures is based on a surfacemediated heterogeneous nucleation process. In the absence of surface ligands (PVP or CA), no heterostructure formed (Figure S4), indicating that PVP or CA molecules on the surface of UCNPs possess regulating ability to direct heterogeneous nucleation and growth of MOFs.
We then investigated the upconversion luminescence (UCL) property of the heterostructures under the irradiation of $980\mathrm{nm}$ laser. As shown in Figure 3a, UCNPs showed three UCL peaks of $\mathrm{Er}^{3+}$ centered around $522~\mathrm{nm}$ $\left({}^{2}\mathrm{H}_{11/2}\mathrm{-}^{4}\mathrm{I}_{15/2}\right)$ , $542~\mathrm{nm}$ ${}^{4}\mathrm{S}_{3/2}$ - $^4\mathrm{I}_{15/2})$ and $655\mathrm{nm}$ $(^{4}\mathrm{F}_{9/2}-{}^{4}\mathrm{I}_{15/2})$ , which overlapped well with the absorption spectrum of the porphyrinic MOFs. The spectra overlap is an indispensable factor for the resonance-based energy transfer (FRET) from the UCNPs to porphyrinic MOFs. Figure 3b presents significantly decreased UCL intensity of
UCDs and UCSs relative to that of UCNPs, suggesting an efficient energy transfer within the two types of heterostructures. Time-resolved photoluminescence measurements showed a significant decrease in the lifetime of UCL at $542\ \mathrm{nm}$ upon the formation of the two types of heterostructures (Figure 3c), further confirming the efficient FRET process. Notably, as compared to UCDs, UCSs possess a much lower UCL intensity and a shorter fluorescence lifetime, indicating higher FRET efficiency from the UCNP to MOF within UCSs than that in UCDs.
Next, SOSG, an indicator of ROS, was used to assess the ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ generation capability of UCSs and UCDs. As shown in Figure 3d, the fluorescence intensity of SOSG in both of UCSs and UCDs dispersions increased rapidly with increased exposure time to $980~\mathrm{nm}$ NIR laser. The results indicated that the NIR light harvesting by the two types of heterostructures enabled ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ generation. As expected, the fluorescence increase of SOSG for UCSs upon irradiation was much higher than that of UCDs under the same irradiation condition (Figure 3d), indicating that UCSs produced more ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ than UCDs. As a control, MOF NPs, UCNPs or a mixture of two types of NPs could not generate ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ under the irradiation of $980\ \mathrm{nm}$ light (Figure S5). Taken together, these results demonstrated that the shortened distance between UCNP and porphyrins within symmetric UCSs in comparison with that in asymmetric UCDs enables improved FRET efficiency and thus enhanced ${}^{1}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ generation capability.
