We then evaluated the cellular internalization of the heterostructures in CT26 cells. As illustrated in Figure S6, after $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ incubation of UCSs or UCDs, the bright MOF fluorescence and UCL can be observed in the cells, indicating the effective cellular uptake of UCSs and UCDs. The ability of UCSs and UCDs to trigger ROS generation in CT26 cells was then evaluated by a fluorogenic marker, 2,7-dichlorofluoresceindiacetate (DCFH-DA). Without NIR light irradiation, the CT26 cells treated with UCSs or UCDs showed minimum fluorescence of DCF (Figure S7). The cells incubated with UCSs followed by NIR irradiation showed high fluorescence of DCF, which is much stronger than that in the cells treated with UCDs and NIR irradiation (Figure 4a). The flow cytometric quantification showed that the cells treated with $\mathrm{UCSs}+\mathrm{NIR}$ induced 1.4-fold higher fluorescence of DCF than that treated with UCDs and NIR irradiation (Figure 4b).
Having verified the high efficiency of NIR light-triggered ROS generation in cells, we next examined the photodynamic cytotoxicity of the heterostructures against CT26 cells by CCK8 assay. Treatment with UCDs or UCSs at different concentrations (up to $100\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ ) resulted in no obvious change in the cell viability (Figure S8), suggesting the good biocompatibility of the NPs. Upon NIR irradiation, UCDs and UCSs exhibited significant cytotoxicity (Figure S9). Of note, irradiated UCSs showed much higher cytotoxicity than that of UCDs under irradiation (Figure S9), confirming the more efficient effect of UCSs as NIR light-induced PDT agents. Therefore, we further encapsulated TPZ, a hypoxia-activatable prodrug, into the pores of the MOF shell of UCSs to achieve the final nanoplatform TPZ/UCSs. The loading amount of TPZ was calculated to be $10\mathrm{wt\%}$ . The in vitro TPZ release profiles from TPZ/UCSs at different $\mathsf{p H}$ values were shown in Figure S10, which displayed an increased release rate at increased acidity.
Next, we investigated the hypoxia-activated cytotoxicity of TPZ/UCSs. $20\%$ (normoxia) and $2\%$ (hypoxia) oxygen levels were chosen for the cell viability assay. As shown in Figure 4c, UCSs without loading of TPZ showed no cytotoxicity in hypoxic conditions. The half-maximal inhibitory concentration $\left(\mathrm{IC}_{50}\right)$ values of TPZ/UCSs to CT26 cells in hypoxic and normoxic condition were 3.02 and $55.04\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ in terms of TPZ (Figure 4d), respectively. These results indicate that TPZ/UCSs have a selective cytotoxicity to cells because of the hypoxiaresponsive activity of TPZ. Notably, incubation of the cells with TPZ/UCSs in hypoxia followed by NIR irradiation (donated $\mathrm{TPZ/UCSs(+)})$ led to higher cytotoxicity $\mathrm{IC}_{50}$ , $0.74~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL})$ than that of the cells treated with TPZ/UCSs in hypoxia without irradiation (donated TPZ/UCSs(-)) $\mathrm{TC}_{50}.$ , $3.02\mathrm{\textrm{~}}\upmu\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{mL})$ , indicating an enhanced cell killing effect through the integration of NIR light-induced PDT and hypoxia-activated chemotherapy. The enhanced anticancer activity of TPZ/UCSs was further supported by Annexin V/PI assay (Figure 4e and Figure S11), which showed that $\mathrm{TPZ/UCSs(+)}$ under hypoxic condition induced a higher numbers of apoptotic cells than those of the control groups.
The effective FRET within UCSs was further evaluated on a cellular level. As represented in Figure 5a, CT26 cells treated with UCSs exhibited much lower green UCL intensity than that incubated with the UCNPs, suggesting the effective FRET from UCNP (the donor) to MOF (the acceptor) within UCSs. To provide more definitive evidence of FRET, fluorescence lifetime imaging microscopy (FLIM) was employed to quantify the change of UCL lifetime of $\mathrm{Er}^{3+}$ emission in CT26 cells. The FLIM is a powerful tool for imaging of fluorescence lifetime independent of fluorescence intensity or imaging depth. As illustrated in Figure 5b, the mean UCL lifetime of green emission of UCNPs was $125~{\upmu\mathrm{s}}$ . In contrast, the mean UCL lifetime of green emission of UCSs decreased to $65~{\upmu\mathrm{s}}$ due to the effective energy transfer from UCNP to MOF. The phasor plot method was used to get quantitative results of the graphic FLIM data. As shown in Figure 5c, two populations marked by two colored circles were shown on the phasor plots, representing the lifetime distributions of UCNPs and UCSs in CT26 cells. As expected, compared with UCNPs, UCSs have a much shorter UCL lifetime. This difference in UCL lifetime can also be observed as a dramatic shift of the UCL lifetime distribution of each pixel (Figure 5d). In addition, the FRET efficacy image of CT26 cells after the treatment with UCSs was presented in Figure 5e, from which we can visually see the relative FRET efficiency levels of UCSs in a single CT26 cell. These results provided direct proofs of efficient FRET process within UCSs in living cells.
Inspired by the good performance of this system in vitro, we next investigate the NIR light-triggered therapeutic effect of TPZ/UCSs on CT26 tumor bearing mice. Thirty-five female BALB/c mice bearing CT26 tumors were randomly divided into seven groups $(\Nu=5)$ ) and treated with PBS, $\mathrm{PBS}(+)$ , TPZ, $\mathrm{UCSs(-)}$ , $\mathrm{TPZ/UCSs(-)}$ , $\mathrm{UCSs}(+)$ , and $\mathrm{TPZ/UCSs(+)}$ , respectively. NIR irradiation $(980\mathrm{\nm},1.2\mathrm{W}/\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ was performed for $20\mathrm{min}$ at $3\mathrm{h}$ post-injection of various agents. As displayed in Figure 6a, rapid tumor growth was observed in the group treated with PBS, $\mathrm{PBS}(+)$ , or UCSs, while tumor growth of mice treated with $\mathrm{TPZ/UCSs(-)}$ was moderately inhibited. The efficiently delayed tumor growth was observed using $\mathrm{UCSs}(+)$ due to solo PDT effect, while $\mathrm{TPZ/UCSs(+)}$ showed the strongest antitumor effect due to the superior combinational efficacy of NIR light-triggered PDT and the hypoxia-activated chemotherapy. On day 14, mice were sacrificed and excised tumors were photographed and weighed. The results confirmed the amplified antitumor efficacy of $\mathrm{TPZ/UCSs(+)}$ (Figure 6b). Similarly, severe nucleus dissociation and necrosis were observed in the H&E stained sections of tumors treated with $\mathrm{TPZ/UCSs(+)}$ , while lower levels of damages were observed for the control groups (Figure 6c). The density of live tumor cells in the group treated with $\mathrm{TPZ/UCSs(+)}$ was significantly decreased $(28.9\%)$ compared with other groups (Figure S12). There is no significant body weight loss for the mice from each group (Figure S13).
It is known that PDT could induce immunogenic cell death (ICD) and, therefore, antitumor immune response via apoptosis and necrosis. The PDT-induced ICD was examined by measuring exposure of calreticulin (CRT) on cell surface. As shown in Figure S14, cells treated with $\mathrm{UCSs}(+)$ or $\mathrm{TPZ/UCSs(+)}$ showed strong green fluorescence of CRT, which is much higher than that on the cells treated with $\mathrm{UCSs(-)}$ or $\mathrm{TPZ/UCSs(-)}$ , suggesting that PDT but not nanomaterial itself yields immunogenic response. The flow cytometric analysis also showed that the cells treated with $\mathrm{UCSs}(+)$ or $\mathrm{TPZ/UCSs(+)}$ displayed significantly enhanced fluorescence of
CRT (Figure S15). Immunostaining confirmed high expression of CRT in the tumors treated with $\mathrm{UCSs}(+)$ or $\mathrm{TPZ/UCSs(+)}$ (Figure S16).
Encouraged by the NIR light-triggered cellular immunity with TPZ/UCSs, we next evaluated whether our nanoplatform could induce a synergistic effect with PD-L1 checkpoint blockade (Figure 7). We tested the abscopal effect on a bilateral CT26 tumor model. The left tumors (primary tumors) were treated with $\mathrm{TPZ/UCSs(+)}$ , while the right tumor (distant tumors) were untreated (Figure 7a). The mice then underwent PD-L1 blockade treatment at day 1, 4, 7, and 10 post NIR triggering. As shown in Figure 7b, c and d, the primary tumors were completely suppressed in the group treated with $\mathrm{TPZ/UCSs(+)}$ or $\mathrm{TPZ/UCSs(+)}$ plus $\mathfrak{a}$ -PD-L1. Importantly, the $\mathrm{TPZ/UCSs(+)}$ with PD-L1 blockade could effectively inhibit the growth of untreated distant tumors on the right side (Figure 7e, f and g), indicating $\mathrm{TPZ/UCSs(+)}$ treatment enhanced the immunotherapy by eliciting consistent abscopal effects. In contrast, there was a slight abscopal effect for the group receiving $\mathrm{TPZ/UCSs(+)}$ or $\mathfrak{a}$ -PD-L1 only. The body weights of mice remained almost unchanged for all the groups, indicating no systemic toxicity (Figure S17).
To further elucidate the underlying immunological mechanism, we investigated the antitumor immunity of CT26 tumor-bearing mice receiving $\mathrm{TPZ/UCSs(+)}$ and $\mathfrak{a}$ -PD-L1 by flow cytometry (Figure 8 and Figure S18). As shown in Figure 8a, the percentage of infiltrating $\mathrm{CD}45^{+}$ cells dramatically increased in the $\mathrm{TPZ/UCSs(+)}$ plus $\mathfrak{a}$ -PD-L1 group in both primary and distant tumors $(22.84\%\pm2.97\%$ and $21.74\%\pm$ $8.32\%$ , respectively) compared to the PBS control group $(8.17\%\pm2.40\%$ and $10.06\%\pm2.85\%$ ). For primary sites, the proportion of infiltrating $\mathrm{CD4^{+}}$ T cells, $\mathrm{CD8^{+}}$ T cells and NK cells in the total tumor cells $(3.15\%\pm1.14\%$ , $2.60\%\pm1.29\%$ and $3.05\%\pm1.11\%$ , respectively) significantly increased after the treatment with $\Gamma\mathrm{PZ/UCSs(+)}$ plus $\mathfrak{a}$ -PD-L1 compared to the PBS control group $(0.63\%\pm0.12\%$ , $0.58\%\pm0.13\%$ and $1.32\%$ $\pm\ 0.18\%$ , respectively) (Figure 8b, c and Figure S19a). For distant sites, there were up to $2.88\%\pm\:1.45\%$ $\mathrm{CD4^{+}}$ T cells , $2.58\%\pm\:1.75\%$ $\mathrm{CD8^{+}}$ T cells and $2.19\%\pm\:0.95\%$ NK cells infiltration in tumors in the combination treatment group, much higher than those of  the PBS control group $(0.88\%\pm0.35\%$ $\mathrm{CD4^{+}T}$ cells, $0.48\%\pm0.20\%\mathrm{CD8^{+}T}$ cells, $0.78\%\pm0.26\%\mathrm{NK}$ cells). Specifically, in mice treated by $\mathrm{TPZ/UCSs(+)}$ with or without $\mathfrak{a}$ -PD-L1, the percentage of infiltrating B cells was increased in both primary and distant tumors (Figure 8d). In addition, an increased ratio of macrophages cells was observed in the group treated with $\mathrm{TPZ/UCSs(+)}$ plus $\mathfrak{a}$ -PD-L1 in both primary and distant tumors (Figure S19b). Taken together, the results demonstrated that TPZ/UCSs-mediated chemo-PDT with checkpoint-blockade therapy enhanced the immunotherapeutic efficiency through the infiltration of effector T cells.
## CONCLUSION
In summary, UCNP@MOFs core-shell heterostructures was synthesized via the controlled growth of MOFs on CA-modified UCNPs. The UCNP core absorbs tissue-penetrating NIR light and then transfer energy to the porphyrins in MOFs shell, enabling efficient NIR-triggered generation of singlet oxygen. Recently, upconverted MOFs based on triplet−triplet annihilation have been developed for shifting their light sensitivity.48,49 However, the synthesis of such upconverted MOFs for NIR light-triggered PDT have not been achieved yet.
Furthermore, by encapsulation of TPZ in the pores of MOF shell, the TPZ/UCSs enables an efficient nanoplatform for combinational NIR-triggered PDT and hypoxia-activated chemotherapy. Moreover, the combined chemo-photodynamic therapy could induce efficient anti-tumor immunity, which allows integration with checkpoint-blockade therapy for inhibition of tumors at distant sites. We envision that this nanoplatform may provide a synergistic comprehensive effect in battling tumor through the integration of NIR-triggered PDT, hypoxia-activated chemotherapy, and cancer immunotherapy.
